## Broadcast-kill tripwire.
##
## Records any real `kill(-1, sig)` — "signal every process I am permitted to
## signal", which on a desktop means the entire graphical session. Nothing in
## normal operation does this, so the rule is near-zero noise and decisive when
## it fires: the audit record names the sending process, its exe, its cmdline
## and its parent, which is exactly what is missing from every other log when a
## session dies.
##
## Motivating incident (2026-08-03): three unexplained GNOME logouts over two
## days, with no crash, no OOM, no coredump and a perfectly orderly teardown in
## the journal. This rule caught `kill -TERM -1` from a project test suite whose
## numeric PID guard had been removed, turning a two-day mystery into a
## one-command answer. See docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md.
##
## a0 is 0xFFFFFFFF and MUST NOT be widened to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. Audit's rule
## field is u32, and on x86-64 a C `int` of -1 is passed via `mov edi,-1`, which
## zero-extends, so the kernel sees 0x00000000FFFFFFFF. A 64-bit constant matches
## nothing — and an audit rule that matches nothing is indistinguishable from a
## machine where nothing bad ever happens. (The rule originally shipped with the
## 64-bit value and only worked because auditctl silently truncated it.)
##
## a1!=0 drops signal-0 probes. Signal 0 performs error checking and sends
## nothing, so it cannot end a session; some test suites legitimately probe with
## kill(-1, 0), and without this filter every such run would leave benign hits.
##
## Expect a burst from systemd-shutdown (pid 1) at every reboot: that is how
## systemd stops the world, and it is benign.
##
## SCOPE: this catches a0 == -1 only. Two neighbouring blast radii are NOT
## covered, deliberately:
##   * kill(0, sig)     — "my whole process group". A real hazard (and named as
##     such in docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md, since `kill -0 "$pid"` succeeds for it
##     too), but shells and job control issue it routinely, so a rule would be
##     noisy enough to train you to ignore the key. Add `-F a0=0x0` only if you
##     are prepared to filter the noise.
##   * kill(-pgid, sig) — one specific foreign process group. Indistinguishable
##     from legitimate process-group teardown without knowing the pgid, so it is
##     not expressible as a static rule.
## -1 is the one that cannot be anything but a session-wide broadcast, which is
## why it is the one worth alerting on.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S kill -F a0=0xFFFFFFFF -F a1!=0 -k logoutsweep
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S kill -F a0=0xFFFFFFFF -F a1!=0 -k logoutsweep
